    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper evaluates purpose-trained concept-bottleneck neurons, not the prevalence or explanatory adequacy of neurons in ordinary pretrained networks addressed by the claim versions.",
    "gate_note": "The reported neuron results concern bottleneck units deliberately trained with concept labels. They do not measure neuron-pole job shares, natural neuron explanations, or whether an explanation predicts activations on new text. The recorded results are related findings about engineered concept neurons and bear on none of the listed versions.",
    "test_reason": "The study builds concept-bottleneck LLMs whose neurons are trained with concept labels to match chosen concepts. It does not audit naturally arising neurons in pretrained models, and it neither replicates nor scores explanations of such neurons. It does not cite or test the essay or the OpenAI explain-neurons work. Its neuron results concern engineered units, so they bear on no version.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly correct. The authors conclude that CB-LLMs are fully automatic, scalable, inherently interpretable LLMs for classification and generation, within about 1% of black-box performance. They also report about 1.5x higher faithfulness ratings than prior work and support steering, concept unlearning and toxicity reduction.",
    "recipe": "none",
    "recipe_differences": "Not a replication or audit of the cited neuron-job claims; the paper trains concept-bottleneck layers using concept labels and evaluates those designed units.",
    "method_sensitivity": "",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Human raters judged the top-activating examples of supervised concept-bottleneck neurons as agreeing with their assigned concept: 4.12 average versus 2.75 for the comparator.",
        "proposed_claim": "Neurons trained with concept supervision in a bottleneck layer are rated by humans as matching their concept on top-activating examples.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r1",
          "r2",
          "r3",
          "r4",
          "r5",
